# Trivy ignore file — HIGH/CRITICAL CVEs we consciously accept, with a reason.
# The deploy Trivy gate uses `ignore-unfixed: true`, so only FIXABLE HIGH/CRITICAL
# reach here. Keep this list tiny and justified; re-check on every pnpm bump.

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# WHEN DOES pnpm ACTUALLY RUN? (read this before writing a new entry)
#
# Most entries below turn on it, so the answer is stated ONCE here rather than
# repeated per entry — three copies of this claim is exactly how it went stale.
#
# CORRECTED 2026-08-28. Three entries used to say "pnpm never runs at runtime
# (the CMD is a direct `node` invocation, no pnpm/sh wrapper)". The Dockerfile
# CMD is indeed direct node, but docker-compose.yml OVERRIDES it for the api
# service with `sh -c '(cd /app && pnpm prisma migrate deploy) && exec node …'`.
# So pnpm DOES run in production — the claim was false as written.
#
# The accurate version, which is what the entries below rely on:
#   - pnpm runs EXACTLY ONCE per container start, before the server exists, as
#     `pnpm prisma migrate deploy`. It resolves an already-installed local bin
#     out of the baked image and performs NO install: no registry fetch, no
#     tarball extraction, no manifest resolution, no lockfile globbing.
#   - It then `exec`s node, replacing itself — pnpm is not a live process while
#     the service handles traffic, so nothing a customer sends can reach it.
#   - At BUILD time pnpm installs with `--frozen-lockfile` from OUR committed
#     lockfile, so no attacker-supplied manifest or tarball reaches it there.
# Each entry still carries its OWN reachability argument on top of this.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

# pnpm — Repository-controlled configDependencies can select a package's native
# install engine. Fixed ONLY in pnpm 11.5.3 (no 10.x backport per Trivy DB).
# We pin pnpm to 10.34.x, which clears every OTHER pnpm CVE from this advisory
# batch (CVE-2026-50015/55487/55698, GHSA-72r4/qrv3). We deliberately stay on
# pnpm 10.x because pnpm 11 (a) drops `pnpm.overrides` from package.json and
# (b) adds a `minimumReleaseAge` policy that rejects the AWS SDK — a larger,
# separate migration.
# Not exploitable in our pipeline: every install runs `--frozen-lockfile`
# against OUR committed lockfile (no attacker-supplied manifest), and we do not
# use `configDependencies`. Revisit when we migrate to pnpm 11.x.
CVE-2026-55697

# corepack's BUNDLED pnpm distribution (/opt/corepack/v1/pnpm/10.34.4/dist/
# node_modules/) vendors its own copies of these. They are NOT in our lockfile,
# so `pnpm.overrides` cannot reach them — the only real fixes are a pnpm release
# that re-bundles them, or dropping corepack from the runtime image (which the
# base stage's `RUN pnpm --version` guard deliberately prevents; see
# apps/api/Dockerfile).
#
# Not exploitable here: both are denial-of-service via attacker-supplied input
# to pnpm ITSELF, and the one pnpm invocation that exists in production installs
# nothing (see the block at the top) — so no attacker-controlled tarball or
# manifest ever reaches its tar/glob code paths.
#
# OUR OWN copies of these packages ARE patched via pnpm.overrides in the root
# package.json (brace-expansion 1.1.16 / 2.1.2 / 5.0.8, js-yaml 4.3.0) — this
# entry covers only the vendored duplicates inside the pnpm bundle.
# Re-check on every pnpm bump; drop these lines once the bundle ships fixes.
CVE-2026-59873
CVE-2026-59874
CVE-2026-13149

# brace-expansion — DoS via a crafted brace pattern.
#
# CORRECTED 2026-08-06. This entry used to claim that 1.1.16 and 2.1.2 were the
# last releases of their lines with no backport, so nothing could be done. That
# expired: 1.1.18, 2.1.4 and 5.0.9 have since shipped, and pnpm.overrides now
# pins all three (`brace-expansion@1` ^1.1.18, `@2` ^2.1.4, `@5` ^5.0.9). Every
# copy in pnpm-lock.yaml is patched — including the archiver@5 / exceljs chain
# the old note called unfixable.
#
# What remains is ONE copy, and only the one: brace-expansion 2.1.1 vendored
# inside corepack's own pnpm bundle (/opt/corepack/v1/pnpm/10.34.4/dist/
# node_modules/), which is not in our lockfile and cannot be reached by
# pnpm.overrides — the same situation as the entries above it.
#
# Not exploitable here: brace-expansion is reached through minimatch/glob doing
# FILE PATH matching, and inside the pnpm bundle those patterns are pnpm's own
# internal globs over our committed lockfile. The one production pnpm invocation
# installs nothing (see the block at the top), so no request body or uploaded
# filename is ever used as a brace pattern.
#
# DROP BOTH LINES once corepack ships a pnpm bundle with a patched copy.
# Re-check on every pnpm bump. (Checked 2026-08-28: pnpm 10.34.5 moves the
# bundled copy to 2.1.2, still short of the fixed 2.1.4 — bumping clears
# nothing, so we stay on 10.34.4.)
CVE-2026-14257
CVE-2026-69152

# ip-address — inconsistent IP parsing leading to SSRF. Fixed in 10.3.1, and
# 10.2.0 is present ONLY inside corepack's pnpm bundle (pnpm vendors it via
# socks-proxy-agent for registry access). It is not in pnpm-lock.yaml — an
# override for it resolves to nothing, which is why one is not added here.
#
# Not exploitable here: the SSRF requires attacker control over the address
# being parsed. The only consumer is pnpm's own proxy stack talking to the
# registry we configure, at BUILD time; the single production pnpm invocation
# reaches no registry at all (see the block at the top), and no customer-supplied
# URL reaches it. The application's own outbound fetches (provider calls,
# outbound webhooks) go through the SSRF-safe fetch in lib/http, which does not
# use this package.
#
# DROP once corepack ships a pnpm bundle on ip-address >= 10.3.1.
# (Checked 2026-08-28: pnpm 10.34.5 still vendors 10.2.0.)
CVE-2026-69192

# tar (node-tar) — DoS via a crafted long-path tar archive. Fixed in 7.5.21.
# Added 2026-08-28, when it began failing every deploy on BOTH images.
#
# Same shape as the corepack entries above, and verified to be so rather than
# assumed: `tar` appears nowhere in pnpm-lock.yaml and nowhere in node_modules —
# the only copy is 7.5.16 vendored inside corepack's pnpm bundle
# (/opt/corepack/v1/pnpm/10.34.4/dist/node_modules/tar), which pnpm.overrides
# cannot reach. It is in the WEB image too for the same reason, not via that
# image's second npm dep tree.
#
# Not exploitable here: node-tar is pnpm's package EXTRACTOR, so reaching it
# needs pnpm to unpack a tarball an attacker crafted. The one pnpm invocation in
# production installs nothing (see the block at the top), and the build-time
# installs take tarballs from our committed lockfile with integrity hashes.
# Nothing in the running service parses a tar archive at all.
#
# Bumping pnpm does NOT fix it today: 10.34.5 (the newest 10.x as of
# 2026-08-28) still bundles tar 7.5.19, below the fixed 7.5.21, and pnpm 11 is
# the separate migration described in the first entry. DROP this line once a
# 10.x bundle ships tar >= 7.5.21 — check with:
#   npm view pnpm@10 dist.tarball | xargs curl -sL | tar -xzO --wildcards \
#     '*/dist/node_modules/tar/package.json' | grep '"version"'
CVE-2026-73566
